course: dictionary
lesson: 6328
title: A word for the driver
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A motorist is a person who drives a car. Read the clues around the word. «Early cars had open tops and roads were mostly dust and stones, so motorists wore goggles, caps, gauntlets and long dust coats.» The sentence talks about cars and dusty roads, so a motorist must be someone who drives a car, not someone who walks.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. "Drivers wore goggles, caps and long dust coats" still makes sense, so you have it right. Motorist is a formal word. It is used in news reports and road signs, like «motorists are warned to slow down».
explain: Look for phrases that show who is meant. «Motorists are advised to take care» tells you the advice is for people who drive. A passing motorist is one driver who goes past you on the road.
fact: A motorist is a person who drives a car.
fact: Formally, a motorist is any road user who drives a motor vehicle.
fact: «Gauntlets» means long gloves.
fact: Early drivers wore goggles, caps and long dust coats because the roads were dusty and open cars let in the wind.
fact: Walkers and cyclists are not motorists, because they are not driving a motor vehicle.
exercise: «Motorists are warned to slow down.» Who is being warned? | Walkers on the pavement | Cyclists on the path | *Drivers | Children playing in the park
exercise: What is a motorist? | A rider on a horse | A person who walks to school | A person who sells motors | *A person who drives a car
exercise: Find and copy one word from the sentence that means long gloves. | coats | goggles | *gauntlets | caps
test: Which of these is NOT a motorist? | A man driving a lorry | A taxi driver | *A cyclist riding to the shop | A woman driving a bus
test: «Motorists are advised to take care in fog.» Who is this advice for? | *People who drive cars | People who sell cars | People who walk dogs | People who fly planes
exam: Is a person walking on the pavement a motorist? | Yes, if they carry a map | *No, a walker is not driving a motor vehicle | Yes, every road user is a motorist | No, because walkers never use roads
